{"name":"Quiet Time","hex":"#b8bcb8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"quiet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["With little or no sound; free of disturbing noise.","Having little motion or activity; calm."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cause (someone or something) to become quiet.","To become quiet or calm."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/quiet"},{"term":"time","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The inevitable progression into the future with the passing of present and past events.","The feeling of the passage of events and their relative duration, as experienced by an individual."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To measure or record the time, duration, or rate of something.","To choose when something commences or its duration."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/time"}],"entries":[{"title":"Quiet time","blocks":[{"text":"Quiet time is a regular individual session of Christian spiritual activities, such as prayer, private meditation, contemplation, worship of God, study of the Bible, or reading of a daily devotional. The term \"quiet time\" or \"sacred time\" is used by 20th-century Protestants, mostly evangelical Christians. Rick Warren points out that it has also been called \"morning watch\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/John_William_Waterhouse_-_The_Missal.JPG/330px-John_William_Waterhouse_-_The_Missal.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quiet_time","wikidata":"Q7271903"},{"title":"Time","blocks":[{"text":"Time is the continuous progression of existence that occurs in an apparently irreversible succession from the past, through the present, and into the future. Time dictates all forms of action, age, and causality, being a component quantity of various measurements used to sequence events, to compare the duration of events or the intervals between them, and to quantify rates of change of quantities in material reality or in conscious experience. Time is often referred to as the fourth dimension and the temporal dimension, in addition to the three spatial dimensions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/MontreGousset001.jpg/330px-MontreGousset001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time","wikidata":"Q11471"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Wooden_hourglass_3.jpg/500px-Wooden_hourglass_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Wooden_hourglass_3.jpg/500px-Wooden_hourglass_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The flow of sand in an hourglass can be used to measure the passage of one hour of time. It also concretely represents the present as being between the past and the future.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWooden_hourglass_3.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Canberra_%28AU%29%2C_Albert_Hall%2C_Sundial_--_2019_--_1740.jpg/500px-Canberra_%28AU%29%2C_Albert_Hall%2C_Sundial_--_2019_--_1740.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Canberra_%28AU%29%2C_Albert_Hall%2C_Sundial_--_2019_--_1740.jpg/1280px-Canberra_%28AU%29%2C_Albert_Hall%2C_Sundial_--_2019_--_1740.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Canberra_%28AU%29%2C_Albert_Hall%2C_Sundial_--_2019_--_1740.jpg/500px-Canberra_%28AU%29%2C_Albert_Hall%2C_Sundial_--_2019_--_1740.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Horizontal sundial in Canberra","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AC